### 5.4.1 The Pinning Contract

The fundamental contract of a buffer manager is that a page given to a client must remain resident and valid until the client signals it is done. In BuzzDB, the `getPage` method is the entry point for this contract.

```cpp
// 15-buzzdb.cpp:399-404
std::unique_ptr<SlottedPage>& getPage(int page_id) {
 auto it = pageMap.find(page_id);
 if (it != pageMap.end()) {
 touch(it->second);
 return it->second->second;
 }
 // ... miss logic follows
```

Both policies scored five hits during the warm-up phase. The difference came after the scan. LRU, which treats all pages equally based only on recency, was completely fooled. The long, sequential scan of pages 6 through 15 methodically pushed every one of the valuable hot pages (1-5) out of the cache. When the workload returned to the hot set, the cache was filled with the five *most recently used* but *least useful* pages (`[15,14,13,12,11]`). The result was five consecutive misses, a catastrophic failure of the cache.

The 2Q policy, in contrast, demonstrated its resilience. During the warm-up, it correctly identified pages 1-5 as having been accessed more than once and promoted them to its protected LRU list. When the scan began, the new pages (6, 7, 8...) were admitted to the FIFO queue. Because these scan pages were never accessed a second time, they were never promoted. The `evict` method in 2Q is designed to be clever:

```cpp
PageID evict() {
 // ...
 if (!FIFO.empty()) {
 evictedPageId = FIFO.front();
 FIFO.pop_front();
 // ...
 } else {
 evictedPageId = LRU.back();
 LRU.pop_back();
 // ...
 }
 // ...
}
```

### 6.6.2 From Policies to Hints

This is where the query plan becomes invaluable. The query executor knows it's about to perform a hash join. It knows the working set will be large. It also knows that a sequential scan has a completely different access pattern. The executor can provide **hints** to the buffer manager, giving it the semantic context it needs to make better decisions.

Consider the access pattern of a sequential scan. The pages are read once and are not needed again. This is a classic case of "no temporal locality." An LRU policy would treat these pages just like any other, placing them at the MRU end of the list. A large scan could therefore "pollute" the entire buffer pool, flushing out valuable, frequently-used index pages. A better approach would be to treat these pages differently. The executor could hint to the buffer manager, "This is a one-time sequential scan. Please use a Most-Recently-Used (MRU) eviction policy for these pages." With an MRU policy, the pages from the scan would be the very first candidates for eviction, protecting the rest of the cache.

This idea requires a richer communication channel between the components that request pages and the buffer manager that serves them. In BuzzDB, our `Policy` interface is simple and abstract:

```cpp
class Policy {
public:
 virtual void touch(PageID page_id) = 0;
 virtual PageID evict() = 0;
 virtual ~Policy() = default;
};
```

